{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the task structure better:\n- Job: 端到端验证 (End-to-end verification) - verify that `rick doing` invokes pi\n- Task1: 端到端验证任务 - verify rick doing command can call pi to execute tasks\n- KR1: 创建一个测试文件 (Create a test file)\n- Test method: 检查目标文件存在 (Check that the target file exists)\n\nThe task goal is to verify that \"rick doing\" command can invoke pi to execute tasks. The test method is \"check the target file exists\". But what is the target file?\n\nHmm, the test method says \"检查目标文件存在\" (Check target file exists). The KR says \"创建一个测试文件\" (Create a test file). So the target file is likely the test script itself: `/workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/.rick/jobs/job_31/doing/tests/task1.py`. Since this is a verification task, the test file's purpose is to check that the whole pipeline works — the fact that the test script itself exists (created by pi invoked via rick doing) proves that rick doing could call pi to execute the task.\n\nWait, but let me think about what the \"target file\" means here. Let me look at how the rick tool works and what other jobs/tests look like to understand the convention. Let me look at previous jobs' test files to see the pattern.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_T6RvoRZleyM6yubq3SwT6686","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick && find .rick/jobs -name \"*.py\" -path \"*tests*\" 2>/dev/null | head -20 && echo \"---\" && find .rick/jobs/job_31 -type d"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_01_E0cylcRoznN6Rgs2ZWdw8398","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick && cat .rick/jobs/job_31/doing/tasks/task1/raw_session_test_gen.log 2>/dev/null | head -50"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":229,"output":484,"cacheRead":8704,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":305,"totalTokens":9417,"cost